WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.470
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:05.440 --> 00:00:07.600
SPEAKER: Web Security
Scanner is a built-in service

00:00:07.600 --> 00:00:09.990
for the Security Command
Center Premium tier.

00:00:09.990 --> 00:00:12.940
It can automatically scan for
and detect key vulnerabilities

00:00:12.940 --> 00:00:15.850
in App Engine, Compute Engine,
and Google Kubernetes Engine

00:00:15.850 --> 00:00:18.740
applications before
deploying them to production.

00:00:18.740 --> 00:00:20.680
There are two types
of scans you can do.

00:00:20.680 --> 00:00:22.420
Managed scans are
configured and managed

00:00:22.420 --> 00:00:24.070
by Security Command Center.

00:00:24.070 --> 00:00:26.240
With an SCC Premium
subscription,

00:00:26.240 --> 00:00:28.510
they're enabled by default
and will automatically run

00:00:28.510 --> 00:00:29.470
once a week.

00:00:29.470 --> 00:00:31.570
Results will appear in
the SCC Vulnerabilities

00:00:31.570 --> 00:00:33.340
tab and related reports.

00:00:33.340 --> 00:00:35.020
With managed scans,
you can centrally

00:00:35.020 --> 00:00:37.840
manage basic web application
vulnerability detection

00:00:37.840 --> 00:00:39.550
for projects in
your organization

00:00:39.550 --> 00:00:42.430
without having to involve
individual project teams.

00:00:42.430 --> 00:00:45.340
If you disable managed scans,
you can re-enable them.

00:00:45.340 --> 00:00:48.520
For this, you need the
SCC and Security Center

00:00:48.520 --> 00:00:52.360
Sources Admin IAM roles,
along with the SCC Premium

00:00:52.360 --> 00:00:53.980
subscription.

00:00:53.980 --> 00:00:56.230
Go to the Sources and
Services Settings page

00:00:56.230 --> 00:00:58.960
and enable managed scans.

00:00:58.960 --> 00:01:01.540
You may need to wait up to
24 hours for the application

00:01:01.540 --> 00:01:03.550
to be detected and scanned.

00:01:03.550 --> 00:01:05.560
Custom scans are also available.

00:01:05.560 --> 00:01:08.350
They provide granular control
over how the scan runs,

00:01:08.350 --> 00:01:11.380
including additional controls
like scanning from a static IP

00:01:11.380 --> 00:01:13.510
or using IAP authentication.

00:01:13.510 --> 00:01:16.270
Before you begin, make sure
the account you're logged into

00:01:16.270 --> 00:01:19.180
has a project editor, the
organization administrator,

00:01:19.180 --> 00:01:22.120
Security Center Admin, and
Security Center Sources Admin

00:01:22.120 --> 00:01:24.370
IAM roles.

00:01:24.370 --> 00:01:26.740
To enable custom scans,
make sure you've also

00:01:26.740 --> 00:01:30.430
enabled Web Security Scanner
in the Settings page.

00:01:30.430 --> 00:01:32.200
To set up a custom
scan, first make

00:01:32.200 --> 00:01:35.608
sure your app is running
with the public URL and IPs.

00:01:35.608 --> 00:01:37.150
Then head to the
Web Security Scanner

00:01:37.150 --> 00:01:38.870
from the navigation bar.

00:01:38.870 --> 00:01:41.060
Select a project in
the page that appears.

00:01:41.060 --> 00:01:43.750
If you haven't enabled the Web
Security Scanner API before,

00:01:43.750 --> 00:01:46.300
you will be prompted to
do so before continuing.

00:01:46.300 --> 00:01:48.920
You are now in the Web
Security Scanner page.

00:01:48.920 --> 00:01:51.760
This page allows you to
create, save, and run a scan.

00:01:51.760 --> 00:01:54.640
Click New Scan to display
the new scan form.

00:01:54.640 --> 00:01:57.670
Add in the URL of the
application you'd like to test.

00:01:57.670 --> 00:02:00.070
You can select authentication
options, including

00:02:00.070 --> 00:02:02.140
needing a Google
account to run the scan

00:02:02.140 --> 00:02:04.540
or using Identity Aware Proxy.

00:02:04.540 --> 00:02:07.330
IAP works by verifying
user identity and context

00:02:07.330 --> 00:02:09.430
of the request to
determine if a user should

00:02:09.430 --> 00:02:11.200
be allowed to access.

00:02:11.200 --> 00:02:13.480
And for now, let's schedule
the scan to run weekly

00:02:13.480 --> 00:02:15.250
and select the starting date.

00:02:15.250 --> 00:02:18.160
You can choose to run scans from
a predefined set of source IP

00:02:18.160 --> 00:02:19.180
addresses.

00:02:19.180 --> 00:02:22.150
This gives the scanner access to
applications behind a firewall,

00:02:22.150 --> 00:02:24.370
but might limit the
scope of the scan.

00:02:24.370 --> 00:02:26.350
The Export box is
automatically checked.

00:02:26.350 --> 00:02:28.630
It allows scan results to
be displayed on the Security

00:02:28.630 --> 00:02:30.790
Command Center dashboard.

00:02:30.790 --> 00:02:33.460
You can also specify the user
agent, which is a web client

00:02:33.460 --> 00:02:35.630
software to be emulated
when scanning your app,

00:02:35.630 --> 00:02:39.310
max scan speed, queries
per second, and risk level.

00:02:39.310 --> 00:02:41.020
Because Web Security
Scanner populates

00:02:41.020 --> 00:02:44.350
fields, pushes buttons, clicks
links, and other interaction,

00:02:44.350 --> 00:02:47.020
low-impact scanning minimizes
requests with a potential

00:02:47.020 --> 00:02:48.730
to modify data.

00:02:48.730 --> 00:02:50.680
Click Save to create the scan.

00:02:50.680 --> 00:02:52.180
The scan will be
queued, and then it

00:02:52.180 --> 00:02:54.010
will be run at a future time.

00:02:54.010 --> 00:02:57.160
It might take several
hours before the scan runs.

00:02:57.160 --> 00:02:59.285
Click Run to schedule the scan.

00:02:59.285 --> 00:03:01.660
The scan is placed in a queue,
and there might be a delay

00:03:01.660 --> 00:03:03.010
before it runs.

00:03:03.010 --> 00:03:06.130
It can take several minutes or
many hours to complete a run,

00:03:06.130 --> 00:03:09.010
depending on the system
load and site complexity.

00:03:09.010 --> 00:03:10.780
Web Security Scanner
will automatically

00:03:10.780 --> 00:03:12.610
crawl your
application, following

00:03:12.610 --> 00:03:15.250
all links within the scope
of your starting URLs

00:03:15.250 --> 00:03:18.010
and attempts to exercise as
many user inputs and event

00:03:18.010 --> 00:03:19.960
handlers as possible.

00:03:19.960 --> 00:03:22.720
The scanner attempts to identify
common web vulnerabilities

00:03:22.720 --> 00:03:25.120
on these pages, including
findings from categories

00:03:25.120 --> 00:03:28.270
in the OWASP Top 10, like
cross-site scripting,

00:03:28.270 --> 00:03:32.950
mixed content, and outdated or
insecure JavaScript libraries.

00:03:32.950 --> 00:03:35.530
The scan will display a
results page when it completes,

00:03:35.530 --> 00:03:38.980
such as the following if no
vulnerabilities were detected.

00:03:38.980 --> 00:03:41.770
Click the scan name to go to
the scan's config page, where

00:03:41.770 --> 00:03:44.410
you'll find more information
about the scan, its history,

00:03:44.410 --> 00:03:47.410
and some controls for running,
editing, or deleting the scan.

00:03:47.410 --> 00:03:49.120
You can see all
vulnerabilities found

00:03:49.120 --> 00:03:51.790
by navigating to the
Vulnerabilities dashboard.

00:03:51.790 --> 00:03:54.760
Filter by vulnerability
type, like OWASP.

00:03:54.760 --> 00:03:56.500
In this case, we have
the highest number

00:03:56.500 --> 00:03:58.960
of cross-site scripting
listings and the recommendation

00:03:58.960 --> 00:04:00.227
provided.

00:04:00.227 --> 00:04:01.810
You can click on a
finding to bring up

00:04:01.810 --> 00:04:03.092
more information about it.

00:04:03.092 --> 00:04:05.050
If we click on this
cross-site scripting error,

00:04:05.050 --> 00:04:07.270
for instance, a pop-up
will display information

00:04:07.270 --> 00:04:09.010
about the finding,
including information

00:04:09.010 --> 00:04:10.690
like the resource
that was flagged,

00:04:10.690 --> 00:04:14.500
how severe the flag is,
and the reproduction URL.

00:04:14.500 --> 00:04:16.860
You can also share the
scan with your team.

00:04:16.860 --> 00:04:19.510
You just completed a scan
using Web Security Scanner.

00:04:19.510 --> 00:04:22.120
You can now set up and run
on-demand, immediate or

00:04:22.120 --> 00:04:24.610
scheduled security scans
for your own application

00:04:24.610 --> 00:04:26.490
from the Google Cloud Console.

00:04:26.490 --> 00:04:29.540
[MUSIC PLAYING]

